# Prefer `watch` over `$effect` for value tracking

When you need to **react to reactive value changes** (sync local state, rebuild derived layout, reset form fields when a prop changes), use `watch` from `runed` — not `$effect`.

`$effect` is an escape hatch for cases that are not “sources → callback on change” (e.g. one-off setup with a custom teardown that `watch` does not model cleanly). Prefer `$derived` / `$derived.by` for computed values with no side effects.

## Pattern

```ts
import { watch } from 'runed';

// ❌ BAD — tracking values with $effect
$effect(() => {
	if (open) {
		name = queue?.name ?? '';
	}
});

// ✅ GOOD — explicit sources + effect callback
watch(
	() => open,
	(isOpen) => {
		if (isOpen) {
			name = queue?.name ?? '';
		}
	}
);
```

Multiple sources: return an array or object from the getter (same pattern as existing `watch` call sites in the app).

```ts
watch(
	() => [open, queue?.id] as const,
	([isOpen]) => {
		if (isOpen) {
			name = queue?.name ?? '';
		}
	}
);
```

## Rules of thumb

| Need | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Side effect when value(s) change | `watch` from `runed` |
| Pure computed value | `$derived` / `$derived.by` |
| Debounced / throttled value | `Debounced` / `Throttled` from `runed` |
| Truly custom effect lifecycle | `$effect` (rare) |

Do not read reactive state inside an `$effect` just to mirror or sync it — that is what `watch` is for.

See also `svelte-5.mdc` (runed utilities) and `reactive-state.mdc` (domain state in `.svelte.ts`).
